## Comparisons

- Ordinal Strand / original C: 1.099× (ratio of medians).
- Ordinal Strand / same-O2 explicit-byte-store C: 1.260× (ratio of medians).
- Original C / same-O2 byte stores: 1.146× (ratio of medians).
- Original C / same-source O2 no-fortify: 1.165× (ratio of medians).
- Ordinal Strand one / eight CPUs: 1.025× (ratio of medians).
- Fingerprint Strand one / eight CPUs: 1.273× (ratio of medians).
- Full-image C serial / unit-parallel eight CPUs: 2.922× (ratio of medians).
- Strand fingerprint minus ordinal, one CPU: 126.89 ms difference of medians; 127.37 ms median within-round difference (all paired differences: 89.14, 147.30, 123.58, 104.67, 142.76, 127.37, 129.41).
- C fingerprint minus ordinal, one CPU (matched full-image path): 107.42 ms difference of medians; 102.45 ms median within-round difference (all paired differences: 108.94, 93.16, 87.93, 130.50, 93.76, 102.45, 130.45).
- Strand fingerprint minus ordinal, eight CPUs: 43.84 ms difference of medians; 44.76 ms median within-round difference (all paired differences: 32.81, 65.86, 43.84, 44.76, 54.82, 37.33, 53.20).

These are whole-process differences, not isolated phase instrumentation.
The affinity column is not a thread count: all reference C variants,
C full/checked/stream/fingerprint remain single-threaded at affinity eight.
The bounded-buffer streaming row does not materialize a full image and is
not directly comparable as a memory-image API. Parallel system time is
summed across threads; it can exceed elapsed time. No claim about real
output-device I/O follows from a `/dev/null` benchmark.

